Hi folks,
I'm comparing 7 species of a genus for a taxonomic study. This genus is divided into two well supported molecular clades (clade A and  B). The sp1 and sp1 belong to clade A. The rest, sp3-sp7 all belong to clade B.
I performed a homogeneity of slopes (HOS) test with the following models, and the output was significant:
fit_unique <- procD.lm(coords ~ log(Csize)*species, SS.type = "III",
                                data = gdf, print.progress = FALSE) # Plot of this model is bellow
fit_common <- procD.lm(coords ~ log(Csize)*species, SS.type = "III",
                                data = gdf, print.progress = FALSE
anova (fit_common, fit_unique)
However when I split my data and analyze each clade separately, the outputs for the HOS test were not significant for both clades.
My question is if a nested model (species/clade or clade/species) would be a more appropriate alternative to splitting the data.
Thanks in advance for any insight.

Best regards

Julio

