Hi Julio,

Note you have an interaction (*) in fit.common "fit_common <- procD.lm(coords ~ log(Csize)*species". However, this seems like a simple typo within the email not affecting your code since you said the ANOVA was significant.

If I understand correctly, you see heterogeneity of slopes when analyzing the combined dataset containing clades A and B. Then, when you analyze just Clade A OR just Clade B the analysis tells you there is homogeneity of slopes within each clade. This makes sense to me if heterogeneity of slopes results from clade-specific differences (if you look at your plot, sp 3-7 have slopes most like each other, compared to sp 1-2). A pairwise test used on the model fit (fit_unique) would help you identify which species differ in slope, my guess is 1:2 differ from 3:7 (or something similar).

A nested term accounting for clade membership, assuming you are interested in differences between clades A and B, would leave out this phylogenetic difference between the clades.

Best,
Bryan

